\end_inset

,
 computed in the same way the Troe formulation.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Time integration strategies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The stability of the CFD time integration may be compromised by explicitly applying the source term of Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "enu:source"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Different approaches can be utilized by time-splitting the evolution of the chemical state and retain the stability of the fluid dynamic algorithms
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Str68,Hou11,Lv15,Joh20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These approaches typically do not involve the flux from either convection or diffusion.
 By removing any spatial fluxes,
 the conservation Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:conservation-law-strong-form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reduces to an ordinary differential equation,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{dy_{f}}{dt}=\mathcal{S}\left(y_{f}\right).\label{eq:conservation-law-to-ode}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By removing the spatial fluxes,
 the fluid state from Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:reacting-navier-stokes-state"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 is reduced to
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{f}=\left(0,\ldots,0,\rho u,C_{1},\ldots,C_{n_{s}}\right)^{}\label{eq:ODE_state}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and by inspecting the source term,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}\left(y_{f}\right)=\left(0,\ldots,0,0,\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{n_{s}}\right)^{},\label{eq:RNS_source}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and analyzing the time rate of change of the internal energy,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\rho u}{\partial t}=\frac{\partial\rho u}{\partial T}\frac{\partial T}{\partial t}+\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}\frac{\partial\rho u}{\partial C_{i}}\frac{\partial C_{i}}{\partial t}=0,\label{eq:derive_omega_T}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the temperature source term is realized as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t}=\omega_{T}=-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}W_{i}u_{i}\omega_{i}}{c_{v}}.\label{eq:temperature_source}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This yields the chemical evolution system solved by ChemGen
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
\frac{dy_{c}}{dt}=S\left(y_{c}\right),\quad & y_{c}=\left(T,C_{1},\ldots,C_{n_{s}}\right),\quad & S\left(y_{c}\right)=\left(\omega_{T},\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{n_{s}}\right).\end{array}\label{eq:extracted_problem-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For brevity we now refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 as the chemicals state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y=y_{c}$
\end_inset

.
 In this section we will cover some techniques implemented in ChemGen to integrate this state in time.
 Isolating the source term as a system of ODEs allows the stiffness of the problem,
 which comes from disparate time scales between slow and fast chemical reactions.
 However,
 these approaches can introduce errors due to time-splitting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formulation presented in Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:extracted_problem-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 is derived using a constant internal energy assumption and thus allows for pressure to change.
 Some systems require a constant pressure assumption.
 Appendix
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "subsec:Constant-Pressure-Strategy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 covers how a constant pressure formulation can be extracted using an assumption that uses a global 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Explicit Runge-Kutta time integration method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
ChemGen provides a Runge-Kutta 4th-order method (RK4) for integrating the chemical state from 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{n}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 over a time interval of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 from Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:extracted_problem-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 This explicit time integration scheme approximates the solution by evaluating the source term at four intermediate stages within each time step
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Hai96"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Given state 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{n}$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 the RK4 method updates to the next state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{n+1}$
\end_inset

.
 The algorithm is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
\Gamma_{1} & = & S\left(y^{n}\right)\\
\Gamma_{2} & = & S\left(y^{n}+\frac{\Delta t}{2}\Gamma_{1}\right)\\
\Gamma_{3} & = & S\left(y^{n}+\frac{\Delta t}{2}\Gamma_{2}\right)\\
\Gamma_{4} & = & S\left(y^{n}+\Delta t\Gamma_{3}\right)\\
y^{n+1} & = & y^{n}+\frac{\Delta t}{6}\left(\Gamma_{1}+2\Gamma_{2}+2\Gamma_{3}+\Gamma_{4}\right)
\end{array}\label{eq:RK4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This method achieves fourth-order accuracy in time,
 meaning the local truncation error scales as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(\Delta t^{5})$
\end_inset

 and the global error as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(\Delta t^{4})$
\end_inset

.
 RK4 is effective for non-stiff problems where high accuracy is desired without the overhead of the implicit solvers,
 which,
 as detailed below,
 require potentially expensive linear solvers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Implicit time integration and Jacobians
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Implicit-Time-Integration"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although explicit methods like RK4 offer high accuracy,
 they can suffer from stability issues,
 particularly for stiff problems.
 In contrast,
 implicit time integration methods provide improved stability at the cost of increased computational overhead.
 Consider first the backward Euler implicit time integration method,
 which seeks 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 that satisfies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{y^{n+1}-y^{n}}{\Delta t}=S\left(y^{n+1}\right).\label{eq:Backward_euler}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Newton's method can be used to find 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 via iteration by solving
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{-y^{n}}{\Delta t}+\frac{y^{n+1}}{\Delta t}-S\left(y^{n+1}\right)=f(y^{n+1})=0\label{eq:BE-rewrite}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and solving the decrement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\delta y^{n+1}=\left(y_{k+1}^{n+1}-y_{k}^{n+1}\right)=-\mathcal{J}\left(y_{k}^{n+1}\right)^{-1}f(y_{k}^{n+1})\label{eq:newtons-method}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G}\left(y_{k}^{n+1}\right)\left(y_{k+1}^{n+1}-y_{k}^{n+1}\right)=-f(y_{k}^{n+1}),\quad f(y_{k}^{n+1})=\frac{y^{n+1}-y^{n}}{\Delta t}-S\left(y^{n+1}\right),\label{eq:newtons-method-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{k+1}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

,
 the state at the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k+1\right)$
\end_inset

th Newton iteration.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{G}\left(y_{k}^{n+1}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the Jacobian for the time integration given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G}\left(y_{k}^{n+1}\right)_{ij}=\frac{\partial f(y_{k,i}^{n+1})}{\partial y_{j}}=\mathcal{D}_{ij}-\mathcal{J}_{ij},\quad\mathcal{J}_{ij}=\frac{\partial S_{i}\left(y_{k}^{n+1}\right)}{\partial y_{j}},\label{eq:jacobian}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{ij}$
\end_inset

 depends on the time integration scheme (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{ij}=\delta_{ij}/\Delta t$
\end_inset

 for the backward Euler method) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is the Jacobian of the chemical source term with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 as the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th element of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th element of of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
ChemGen employs analytical differentiation to evaluate the chemical source term Jacobian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 The code generation is optimized to compute only necessary components for efficient matrix assimilation.The elements of the species concentration source term Jacobian are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\omega_{k}\left(y\right)}{\partial C_{j}}=\frac{\partial\omega_{k}}{\partial C_{j}}+\frac{\partial\omega_{k}}{\partial T}\frac{\partial T}{\partial C_{j}},\label{eq:derivative_matrices}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial C_{j}}=-\left(\frac{\partial\rho u}{\partial T}\right)^{-1}\frac{\partial\rho u}{\partial C_{j}}=-\frac{1}{\rho c_{v}}W_{j}u_{j}=-\frac{W_{j}u_{j}}{\rho c_{v}}.\label{eq:temperature_wrt_species}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

With these derivatives known an 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{s}+1$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{s}+1$
\end_inset

 matrix is formed,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}_{ij}=\begin{cases}
\frac{\partial\omega_{T}}{\partial T} & j=1\\
\frac{\partial\omega_{T}}{\partial C_{j-1}} & i=1;\quad j>1\\
\frac{\partial\omega_{i-1}}{\partial T} & i>1;\quad j=1\\
\frac{\partial\omega_{i-1}}{\partial C_{j-1}} & i>1;j>1
\end{cases},\label{eq:derivative_matrices_temp_evol}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\omega_{T}}{\partial T}=-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}W_{i}\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial T}\omega_{i}}{c_{v}}+\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}W_{i}\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial T}\omega_{i}}{c_{v}^{2}}\frac{\partial c_{v}}{\partial T}\label{eq:temperature_source_2-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\omega_{T}}{\partial C_{j-1}}=-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}W_{i}\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial T}\frac{\partial\omega_{i}}{\partial C_{j-1}}}{c_{v}}+\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}W_{i}u_{i}\omega_{i}}{c_{v}^{2}}\frac{\partial c_{v}}{\partial C_{j-1}}.\label{eq:temperature_source_2-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Additional time integrators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While the backward Euler time integration is inherently stable,
 it may exhibit poor accuracy or convergence for certain chemical models and time step sizes.
 Therefore,
 ChemGen offers additional implicit integrators,
 as listed in Table
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Time_integrators"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

