\begin_layout Standard
Other time integration strategies not yet implemented in ChemGen can be added using the same framework as the existing methods.
 These include,
 but are not limited to,
 multi-step implicit methods such as backward differentiation formulas,
 which utilize information from previous time steps,
 and higher-order Rosenbrock methods 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Zha14"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which require additional stages.
 Integration methods such as DGODE 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Joh20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and CHEMEQ2
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Mot01"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 may be more challenging to implement.
 DGODE is based on finite element numerics,
 while CHEMEQ2 requires the source term to be separated into distinct production and consumption components,
 introducing additional complexities not currently addressed by ChemGen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Linear solvers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The aforementioned implicit time integrators require the solution to the linear system
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:newtons-method-2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 recast here as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Ax=b.\label{eq:matrix_example_1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

It is well-established that employing a direct solver requires 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 operations for an 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

 square matrix
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Gol13"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Although ChemGen supplies a direct solver,
 to address the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 cost,
 its default linear solver is the Generalized Minimal Residual (GMRES) method
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Saa86"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which is implemented as a standalone function.
 GMRES is an iterative Krylov subspace method that builds an orthonormal basis of the Krylov subspace and minimizes the residual over this subspace at each iteration.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The number of GMRES iterations can be reduced by applying a preconditioner,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

,
 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
M^{-1}Ax=M^{-1}b.\label{eq:matrix_example_preconditioner}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

ChemGen provides two standard options:
 Jacobi preconditioning and Gauss-Seidel preconditioning.
 In the former,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 simply consists of the diagonal of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 which can be efficiently inverted by computing the reciprocal of each diagonal entry.
 In the latter,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 consists of the lower triangular portion of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 (including the diagonal),
 which can be easily applied with forward substitution.
 For more advanced preconditioning techniques used in chemical kinetics,
 see,
 for example,
 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Wal23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
ChemGen is not intended to serve as a comprehensive linear-solver library.
 For more advanced or scalable linear solver capabilities,
 ChemGen can be interfaced with external libraries such as Eigen
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Gue10"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or PETSc
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Bal97"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which offer efficient preconditioned Krylov solvers and direct sparse solvers for large systems.
 Additionally,
 while ChemGen includes several implicit time integration strategies (e.g.,
 backward Euler,
 SDIRK methods),
 offloading the integration of stiff ODE systems to established libraries like CVODES
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Ser05"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is also a reasonable approach.
 Such packages allow user-supplied source terms and Jacobians that can then be used in time integration strategies.
\end_layout

