\begin_layout Standard
To test the source term accuracy,
 we randomly generate a chemical state as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
T & = & 1000+r_{T}1500\text{ K}\nonumber \\
p & = & 0.1+9.9r_{p}\text{ atm}\nonumber \\
X_{i} & = & r_{i},\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{s},\label{eq:chemical_state_01}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{T},r_{p},r_{1},\dots,r_{n_{s}}\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$
\end_inset

,
 which denotes a uniform distribution.
 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the mole fraction of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th species,
 defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
X_{i}=\frac{C_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}C_{i}},\label{eq:mole_fraction}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is renormalized after all 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{s}$
\end_inset

 species are randomly generated.
 We then calculate the concentrations as 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}=X_{i}p/\left(RT\right)$
\end_inset

,
 forming the complete chemical state for ChemGen,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{c}$
\end_inset

.
