\begin_layout Plain Layout
In computational physics,
 The optimal choice of time integrator is problem dependent.
 For unsteady simulations requiring extremely small time steps,
 such as detonations,
 the YASS integrator is often sufficient,
 offering a balance of stability,
 accuracy,
 and computational efficiency.
 In contrast,
 higher-order methods like SDIRK-4 may be more suitable for steady-state or low Mach number solvers,
 where larger time steps are feasible.
 Although lower-order integrators are generally faster per step,
 they may necessitate sub-stepping or adaptive routines to maintain stability and accuracy,
 introducing additional complexity that may not be desirable in all applications.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To assure the correct implementation of these implicit methods beyond comparisons to Cantera,
 we assess order of convergence of the YASS,
 SDIRK-4,
 and Rosenbrock methods.
 The implementation of SDIRK-2 is similar to SDIRK-4 and Backward Euler is YASS with more linear solver iterations.
 Therefore,
 we omit them from the discussion here.
 We employ the extracted hydrogen submodel from FFCM-2 in a homogeneous reactor simulation with initial conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
T & = & 1800\text{ K}\nonumber \\
p & = & 101325Pa\nonumber \\
\left(X_{O_{2}},X_{H_{2}},X_{N_{2}}\right) & = & \left(0.2,0.2,0.6\right).\label{eq:chemical_state_04}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We utilize the state at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=4\;\mu$
\end_inset

s,
 which is the approximate location of maximum 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial T}{\partial t}$
\end_inset

.
 This location in time represents the approximate location in the solution where the chemical state changes the most.
 We selecte 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t=2\times10^{-6}$
\end_inset

 for SDIRK-4 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t=1\times10^{-7}$
\end_inset

 for YASS and Rosenbroc.
 We perform five refinements that change the integration time step,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

,
 by a factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h=\Delta t/2^{r}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $r=0\dots4$
\end_inset

.
 We use the finest solution,
 corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $h=\Delta t/16$
\end_inset

,
 as the reference solution when computing the error,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\epsilon_{h}=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}\left(C_{h,i}-C_{\frac{\Delta t}{16},i}\right)^{2}.\label{eq:L2_species}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of the convergence study are summarized in Figure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:convergence_study"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 The dashed lines represent the theoretical convergence rates.
 In all three cases,
 the expected convergence rates are recovered.
\end_layout

