\begin_layout Subsection
ChemGen in a detonation simulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To demonstrate the usage of ChemGen in an existing computational framework,
 we target the chemistry evaluation in detonationFoam
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sun23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 which is based on the OpenFOAM package
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Ope25"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 we focus on the 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

detonationEulerFoam
\end_layout

\end_inset

 solver,
 which does not include viscous effects.
 The solver treats each individual species conservation equation via a segregated approach by solving
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\rho Y_{i}}{\partial t}+\nabla_{\mathrel{FVM}}\cdot\mathcal{F}\left(\rho Y_{i}\right)=\mathcal{R}_{i},\label{eq:openfom_fvm}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\mathrel{FVM}}\cdot\mathcal{F}\left(\rho Y_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes the finite volume discretization of the species flux and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an applied source term of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th specie.
 For the complete set of conservation equations and additional details on the computational strategy,
 see
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sun23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 .
 Here the source term is calculated as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}=\left(\frac{C_{i}^{n+1}-C_{i}^{n}}{\Delta t}\right)W_{i}\label{eq:openfom_RR}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and is held constant during each species equation solve.
 In Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:openfom_RR"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the molecular weight,
 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}^{n}$
\end_inset

 are the current concentrations calculated from the current thermodynamic state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 is the desired fluid time step for integrating Equation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:openfom_fvm"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 are the predicted concentrations from solving the species evolution ODEs,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial y_{c}}{\partial t}=S(y_{c})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a linearized production term estimated from solving the detailed kinetics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In detonationFoam
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sun23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the seulex ODE solver
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Hai96"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 supplied by OpenFOAM is used to solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

.
 The seulex solver utilizes an extrapolation algorithm based on the implicit Euler method with step size control and order selection.
 Due to the small time step size 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t=1\times10^{-9}$
\end_inset

 s we replace the seulex solver with ChemGen's YASS integrator combined with Jacobi-preconditioned GMRES.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We consider a two-dimensional detonation test that has been simulated in many previous studies (see Oran et al.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Ora98"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Houim and Kuo
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Hou11"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Lv and Ihme
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Lv15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Johnson and Kercher
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Joh20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and Ching et al.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Chi22_2"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 The domain here,
 which differs slightly from those in the aforementioned works,
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $0.5$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

m long (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

-direction) with a channel height of 
\begin_inset Formula $0.06$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

m (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

-direction).
 The initial mesh is a uniform quadrilateral mesh with 
\begin_inset Formula $2000$
\end_inset

 cells in the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

-direction and 
\begin_inset Formula $240$
\end_inset

 cells in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

-direction.
 We use the AMR default settings from
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sun23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 resulting in three possible refinements,
 with a minimum mesh size of 
\begin_inset Formula $31\times10^{-6}$
\end_inset

 m.
 We use the slip wall condition for all boundaries.
 We again utilize the hydrogen submodel extracted from FFCM-2
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Zha23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The initial conditions are based on a steady detonation solution from the Shock & Detonation Toolbox
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "sdtoolbox"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 where the pre-shock state is given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad v_{1} & = & 0\text{ m/s},\\
\quad\quad\quad\;X_{Ar}:X_{O_{2}}:X_{H_{2}} & = & 7:1:2\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad p & = & 6670\text{ Pa}\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad T & = & 298\text{ K.}
\end{array}\label{eq:detonation-1d-initialization}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In addition,
 we perturb the detonation front such that it varies in the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

-direction.
 In Sun et al.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sun23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and in this work a sinusoidal profile is specified to perturb the front location,
 giving rise to instabilities that then transition to the expected solution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The initial front progresses into the unreacted flow region,
 and the irregular structures from the initialization cause the development of two-dimensional fluid dynamic features eventually leading to an established two-dimensional cellular structure.
 These structures were visualized using a maximum-pressure history,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*,n+1}(x)=\max\left\{ p^{n+1}(x),p^{*,n}(x)\right\} $
\end_inset

 shown in Figure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:pressure-max-value"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=200\text{ }\mu$
\end_inset

s.
 These results were calculated by 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

detonationEulerFoam
\end_layout

\end_inset

 using ChemGen for the chemistry and demonstrate that ChemGen yields the expected cellular detonation structures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figures
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:detonation_T"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
