\end_inset

,
 are also reported.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reduction in error with increasing polynomial degree provides additional confidence in the correctness of the ChemGen implementation.
 We also found that several parameters in the UCSD model,
 particularly those used in Troe fall-off reactions,
 contain extremely large or small values.
 For instance,
 in reaction 9,
 the parameters are specified as 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
texttt{T3:
 1.0e-30,
 T1:
 1.0e+30}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 .
 The interpretation and handling of such extreme values can vary between software implementations.
 Examining 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{\mathrm{cent}}$
\end_inset

,
 given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F_{\mathrm{cent}}(T)=(1-\alpha_{j})\exp(-T/T_{j,3})+\alpha\exp(-T/T_{j,1})+\exp(-T_{j,2}/T),
\]

\end_inset

we note that the very small value of 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{3}$
\end_inset

 effectively drives the first term to zero,
 while the extremely large value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{T_{1}}$
\end_inset

 drives the second exponential term to one.
 It is assumed,
 based on the specification,
 that omitting 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

 entirely implies that the third term is excluded from the calculation,
 rather than setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{T_{2}=0}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "cantera"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Updating ChemGen to address these edge cases and avoid numerical instabilities led to reduced error and recovered the expected trend that higher-order polynomial fits improved accuracy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Source term Jacobian error 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:jacobianErel"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Niemeyer et al.
 verified the PyJac-generated Jacobian by comparing their analytical Jacobian to a differentiated Jacobian across a wide range of thermochemical states,
 evaluating between 100,000 and 1 million samples depending on the chemical model
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Nie17"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 They reported two metrics that could be used per chemical state to evaluate the relative error,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{rel}=||\frac{\mathcal{J}_{ij}-\hat{\mathcal{J}_{ij}}}{\hat{\mathcal{J}_{ij}}}||{}_{F}=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{s}}\left(\frac{\mathcal{J}\left(C_{i},T\right)_{ij}-\hat{\mathcal{J}}\left(C_{i},T\right)_{ij}}{\hat{\mathcal{J}_{}}\left(C_{i},T\right)_{ij}}\right)^{2}}\text{and}\label{eq:E_rel}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{norm}=\frac{\|\mathcal{J}_{ij}-\hat{\mathcal{J}_{ij}}\|{}_{F}}{\hat{\|\mathcal{J}_{ij}}\|{}_{F}}=\frac{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{s}}\left(\mathcal{J}_{}\left(C_{i},T\right)_{ij}-\hat{\mathcal{J}}\left(C_{i},T\right)_{ij}\right)^{2}}}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n_{s}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{s}}\left(\hat{\mathcal{J}}\left(C_{i},T\right)_{ij}\right)^{2}}}.\label{eq:E_norm}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In these error metrics,
 only matrix elements where 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mathcal{J}_{ij}|>\hat{||\mathcal{J}_{b}}||_{F}/10^{20}$
\end_inset

 are considered,
 where the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 denotes the Frobenius norm,
 i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $||A||_{F}=\sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(A_{ij}\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

 for matrix
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 of size 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times m$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Niemeyer et al.,
 it was reported that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{rel}$
\end_inset

 may be more useful in identifying discrepancies in both larger and smaller matrix elements.
 Table
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "tab:jacobian_norms"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reports the mean and maximum error norms for both 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{rel}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{norm}$
\end_inset

 using ChemGen for five chemical models with 10,000 randomly generated chemical states.
 Each analytical Jacobian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{ij}$
\end_inset

,
 is compared to the second order finite difference Jacobian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathcal{J}_{ij}}$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta C=10^{-6}$
\end_inset

 Kmol/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta T=10^{-3}$
\end_inset

 K.
 Overall,
 both error norms suggest that the analytical Jacobian is producing the expected derivatives.
 In this study,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{rel}$
\end_inset

 errors are generally higher,
 but never exceeding 
\begin_inset Formula $3\times10^{-3}$
\end_inset

.
 Figure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "fig:jacobian_error_distribution"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 shows the distributions of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{norm}$
\end_inset

 (left) and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{rel}$
\end_inset

 (right).
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{norm}$
\end_inset

 distributions are orders of magnitude smaller than the 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{rel}$
\end_inset

 distributions.
 Regardless,
 these results give confidence in the ability to accurately calculate the analytical Jacobians.
\end_layout

